Autism Spectrum Disorder
Paediatric & Neonatal
Social communication deficits, Repetitive stereotypic behaviors, Restricted rigid interests

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-007
Reason for Visit: Acute, unmanageable behavioral crisis and profound emotional distress following an environmental disruption, requiring immediate assessment to rule out physical trauma or acute medical illness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, prolonged, inconsolable screaming and stereotypic physical motor movements lasting over three hours. Immediate assessment was required to exclude acute occult physical pain, acute encephalopathy, and to stabilize a volatile, unsafe home environment prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Their entire schedule changed this afternoon, and they have been rocking and screaming for hours; absolutely nothing will calm them down."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with extreme, profound distress distinctly triggered by a micro-change disrupting their strictly enforced rigid daily routine earlier today. The caregiver reports a sudden onset of frantic rocking and excessive hand-flapping. The patient has an established history of persistent deficits strictly in normal reciprocal social communication and extreme hyper-reactivity to sensory inputs. The current episode is unremitting and has escalated into self-injurious behavior (head-banging). There is a complete lack of spontaneous complex social communication to articulate the source of distress.
Functional Status: Currently entirely dysregulated, unable to engage in basic functional tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of physical injury due to repetitive head-banging and severe caregiver emotional exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 125 | Temp: 36.9C | RR: 26 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 35 Kg
General: Highly agitated, avoiding eye contact completely, engaging in continuous rhythmic rocking.
Physical Exam: Observation confirms prominent social communication deficits and repetitive stereotypic behaviors (hand-flapping). Neurological examination, limited by cooperation, reveals normal gross motor power and tone. Abdominal palpation is soft and non-tender, ruling out acute abdomen. Tympanic membranes are clear bilaterally, ruling out acute otitis media. There are restricted rigid interests evident by the patient tightly clutching a specific mechanical toy part throughout the exam. No acute stigmata of traumatic injury noted on the head.
Environmental Observations: Loud television running in the background, harsh fluorescent lighting overhead.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular psychotropic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Autism Spectrum Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Behavioral Crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Occult Infection (e.g., Otitis Media, UTI) - Ruled out due to afebrile status, clear tympanic membranes, and absence of localizing signs of pain upon gentle palpation.

Acute Encephalopathy - Ruled out due to the preservation of the patient's baseline restricted rigid interests and the clear environmental trigger precipitating the event.

Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal Infections (PANDAS) - Ruled out due to the chronicity of the underlying traits and absence of acute choreiform movements or sudden onset tic disorder.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a prolonged, unmanageable behavioral crisis with self-injurious actions mandated urgent after-hours physical assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the critical need to exclude an occult medical etiology—such as acute otitis media or a surgical abdomen—presenting atypically in a child with profound social communication deficits. Objective examination successfully ruled out acute organic pathology and confirmed severe sensory and routine-disruption overload. Delaying care until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in a state of extreme distress, increasing the risk of significant self-harm and completely destabilizing the family unit. Immediate physical assessment and environmental modification provided crucial acute stabilization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: No acute pharmacological sedation indicated at this time. Focus on profound environmental de-escalation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immediately provide a highly preferred, restricted-interest item directly following 30 seconds of reduced screaming or cessation of head-banging. This explicitly reinforces the operant pathway of self-soothing and calm physical presentation over violent behavioral acting out.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to behavioral therapy team for crisis management strategies.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommend padded floor mats for the patient's designated safe zone.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to discuss long-term behavioral regulation strategies and potential psychiatric review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient engages in severe self-injurious behavior resulting in bleeding, loss of consciousness, or if caregivers are physically unable to prevent imminent harm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis represents an acute crisis superimposed on a chronic developmental condition; behavioral trajectories are unpredictable.
Clinical Course: The acute dysregulation will slowly subside once the environmental sensory load is minimized and a predictable routine is forcefully re-established.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the patient sustains head trauma with vomiting, lethargy, or if physical safety cannot be maintained at home.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The current distress is a physiological panic response to routine disruption and sensory overload, not willful defiance.
Actionable Steps: Immediately turn off the television and dim the lights. Limit verbal commands to one or two words. Do not attempt to physically restrain the rocking behavior unless self-injury is imminent.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Acute sensory/behavioral crisis secondary to ASD. Organic illness ruled out. Implement strict low-sensory environment. See GP in 48h.

SMS EDUCATION

Reward 30 seconds of calm immediately with a preferred item. Dim lights and reduce noise. Go to ED if severe self-injury occurs.
